  "strategy": "**Approach and Rationale**\nWe will use a constrained optimization method with a dual-acceleration system to minimize the objective function. The core idea is to treat the minimization of the maximum convolution term as a bandpass filtering problem, projecting the sequence into a frequency namespace where energy concentration issues become separable. This differs from previous approaches by avoiding gradient-based optimization for high-dimensional spaces and moving to an elementary harmonic conjugation.\n\nOur mathematical formulation uses:\n1. A sequence a represented in the time domain\n2. Its Fourier transform F (mode 'full')\n3. The squared magnitudes |F[k]|\u00b2, requiring minimization of max(|F[k]|\u00b2)\n\n**Implementation Procedure**\nWe will implement a three-stage optimization:\n1. **Frequency namespace warm-up**: Generate several candidate sequences for fixed lengths 512 and 1024 using harmonic-based sampling.\n2. **Hadamard development stage**: Either use a provided baseline sequence (if available) or start with all-one sequences at length 512.\n3. **Structured perturbation refinement**: When optimizing a sequence of length n \u2260 512, pad or truncate to 512 and then min\n  For each n:\n  \u2022 Create a sequence: hr\u0131r of length n using [cos(2\u00b7\u03c0\u00b7k\u00b7t) pattern with random amplitudes\n  \u2022 Normalize logarithmically (a=filters*original)\n  \u2022 Apply gradient-based optimization using numerical central differences and L1 minimization schedules\n  \u2022 Output overall minimum sequence found across all candidate lengths\n\n**Computational Efficiency and Length Choices**\nWe choose fixed length comparisons and normalization schedules:\n- Each candidate length runs independently\n- For hours the best sequence must be all non-negative and have valid normalizations\n- The optimized sequences are evaluated for n in [512, 1024]\n- Time budget: 1500 seconds\n\n**Fallback Design**\nIf the optimization process fails or times out, we return a fallback of optimized_all_ones at length 512.",
